## 1. Cache-Control per resource type (straight from the origin)
   concept: the origin decides who may store each response and for how long; a shared cache and a browser read the same header differently (s-maxage and private are for shared caches)
   hashed asset       /assets/app.503f4b48f9.css       200  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable  ETag: "COKjZjo4LWezoMGF"
   personal (JSON)    /api/me                          200  Cache-Control: private, no-cache  ETag: "FRJjvNewbMQxIMEq"
   personal (page)    /members/me                      200  Cache-Control: private, no-cache  ETag: "VAA354ia61wAHgvz"
   shared reference   /api/funds                       200  Cache-Control: public, max-age=0, s-maxage=300, stale-while-revalidate=60  Cache-Tag: funds  ETag: "funds-v1"
   bilingual page     /help/contributions              200  Cache-Control: public, max-age=60, s-maxage=600  Vary: Accept-Language  ETag: "JciQ6oUqex6qRycG"
   ok   hashed assets are public, a year, immutable
   ok   personal data is private, no-cache, with an ETag
   ok   shared reference data: s-maxage with stale-while-revalidate, an ETag and a tag to purge by
   ok   the bilingual page says it varies by Accept-Language, and answered in French

## 2. Conditional GET: 304 Not Modified
   concept: the client sends back the ETag it has in If-None-Match; if nothing changed the origin answers 304 with no body
   /api/me with If-None-Match "FRJjvNewbMQxIMEq": 304, 0 body bytes (the 200 was 117)
   /api/funds 200 in 137.2 ms (2734 bytes); with If-None-Match "funds-v1": 304 in 4.5 ms (the ETag is the data version: no query to run)
   ok   both answer 304 with an empty body
   ok   the version-based ETag makes the 304 cheaper than the 200
   Chromium visited /members/me, /help/contributions, /members/me; the origin saw: {"/members/me 200":1,"asset 200":2,"/help/contributions 200":1,"/members/me 304":1}
   ok   the browser fetched each asset once (immutable), and revalidated its private page (304) instead of downloading it again

